-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Replacement Policy
+
+The cache uses 2Q replacement so that one-use sheets from a long screening cannot displace sheets that editors revisit. All replacement state is private to the worker and is configured by worker settings only.
+
+Three structures track sheets:
+
+- **Probation queue**: a FIFO of resident sheets rendered once and not yet re-rendered after leaving probation.
+- **Protected queue**: an LRU of resident sheets that were rendered again after being evicted from probation, which shows reuse beyond a single viewing burst.
+- **Ghost list**: a FIFO of cache keys only, with no JPEG bytes, for sheets recently evicted from probation.
+
+Behavior:
+
+- A completed render whose key is in the ghost list is admitted to the protected queue and its ghost entry is removed. Any other completed render is admitted to the probation queue.
+- A cache hit on a protected sheet moves it to the most-recently-used end. A cache hit on a probation sheet does not reorder or promote it, so the repeated requests of one pending-then-ready viewing do not count as reuse.
+- When eviction is needed, the oldest probation sheet is evicted if the probation queue exceeds its byte budget; otherwise the least recently used protected sheet is evicted. A sheet evicted from probation has its key appended to the ghost list; a sheet evicted from the protected queue is forgotten.
+- The probation byte budget is a worker setting, initially 3 GiB (25% of the cache). The ghost list holds a bounded number of keys, initially 3,000 (the sheets that fit in half the cache at the typical 2 MiB size). Both are expected to be tuned against observed workloads.
+- The two queues share the single 12 GiB bound. The probation budget only decides which queue is evicted from first.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Replacement policy | 2Q | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Long sequential screenings produce many one-use sheets beside a small, repeatedly compared working set. LRU lets a scan evict the comparison sheets. Aged frequency counts retain popular clips but need counter maintenance and can keep an earlier project's favorites too long. 2Q resists scans at the cost of two queues, a ghost list, and a probation-size setting. None of the three changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget. |
+| Promotion trigger | Re-render after probation eviction (ghost-list hit) | Promote on a second hit while still in probation | Requests for one sheet cluster within a viewing burst, including retries after a 202. Counting those as reuse would let a single scan promote every sheet it touches. |
+| Ghost list contents | Keys only | Keep evicted JPEG bytes | Keys cost negligible memory and no disk, so the 12 GiB bound is unaffected. |
